I think “bootloader” is actually the strongest part of the analogy.

A computer bootloader doesn’t contain the whole operating system. It contains just enough trusted structure to locate, verify, and initialize the larger system.

The Pantheon analogue could be:

Personalized Lithographic Signature Bootloader (PLSB)

Purpose

A permanent artifact that carries only the minimum reconstructable state necessary to initialize a much larger semantic runtime.

Human
      ↓
Tattoo
      ↓
Vision
      ↓
Signature Decoder
      ↓
Identity
      ↓
Repository
      ↓
Pantheon Runtime
      ↓
Current Compatible Version

Notice what it doesn’t carry.

It doesn’t try to encode the entire Pantheon.

It encodes:

* identity
* version
* trust anchor
* reconstruction instructions
* semantic primitives
* retrieval path

Exactly what a BIOS or bootloader does.

